  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "If the line \\(y=mx+1\\) intersects the ellipse \\(x^2+4y^2=1\\) exactly once, then the value of \\(m^2\\) is:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{2}{3}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{3}{4}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{4}{5}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{5}{6}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": "3/4"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1971-p19.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Substitution gives \\[ (1+4m^2)x^2+8mx+3=0. \\] Tangency requires its discriminant to vanish: \\[ 64m^2-12(1+4m^2)=0. \\] Thus \\(16m^2=12,\\) so \\(m^2=\\frac{3}{4}.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Substitute the line equation into the ellipse",
        "Exactly one intersection means the resulting quadratic has discriminant zero"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
